"Shows individual numeric observations in category strips, using only perpendicular jitter to reduce occlusion.\n\nUse when: Small or medium samples deserve direct inspection of spread and outliers. Group summaries would conceal unusual observations or sample imbalance.\n\nAvoid when: The sample is so dense that individual marks cannot be distinguished.\n\nVisual grammar: Position along the numeric axis encodes the measured value. Perpendicular position identifies the group and optionally provides nonnumeric jitter.\n\nBuild: Assign one strip to each group and retain all valid observations. Place observations at their exact numeric positions. Apply modest seeded perpendicular jitter and add group sample sizes.\n\nCheck: Check jitter never changes the numeric coordinate. Verify all categories share the same units and scale. Confirm repeated observations remain represented even when their points overlap.\n\nPitfalls: Jitter spread is not another measured variable. Use a beeswarm or histogram when overlapping marks obscure the observation count.\n\nExample: Display synthetic completion times for three training groups, with one small dot per participant.\n\nAlternatives: Beeswarm plot, Box plot, Raincloud plot."
